1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,361
Someone said A.I. can't come up with a brand new idea.

2
00:00:03,561 --> 00:00:10,081
Someone replied: sure it can. Put every Steam game on a map, find the empty spots, and turn them back into ideas.

3
00:00:10,281 --> 00:00:13,128
So I tested it. It worked, just not the way they said.

4
00:00:13,548 --> 00:00:20,925
First, I downloaded every game's page from Steam, the biggest P.C. game store. All one hundred and ninety-five thousand.

5
00:00:21,346 --> 00:00:27,515
Then an A.I. turned each game's description into a point on a map, so similar games land close together.

6
00:00:27,715 --> 00:00:28,876
That's called an embedding.

7
00:00:29,076 --> 00:00:32,267
Two zombie shooters? Neighbors. A farming game? Far away.

8
00:00:32,687 --> 00:00:37,044
Here's the map. Every dot is a real Steam game, and genres form neighborhoods.

9
00:00:37,464 --> 00:00:41,236
To score an idea, I put it on the map and look at its ten closest games.

10
00:00:41,436 --> 00:00:44,165
The farther away they are, the more unusual the idea.

11
00:00:44,585 --> 00:00:46,100
Can new wording fake a high score?

12
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:51,936
No. When the A.I. rewrote real games in totally new words, most landed near the originals.

13
00:00:52,356 --> 00:00:55,444
Then I found empty spots on the map, far from every game.

14
00:00:55,644 --> 00:01:00,062
For each one, an A.I. wrote a game idea and kept rewriting it to land there.

15
00:01:00,482 --> 00:01:05,762
For comparison, I also just asked the same A.I.: give me game ideas nobody has made.

16
00:01:06,182 --> 00:01:08,107
Surprise: the empty spots lost.

17
00:01:08,307 --> 00:01:17,289
For a typical idea from just asking, about sixteen thousand Steam games were more unusual. For the empty spots, fifty-three thousand. Fewer is better.

18
00:01:17,489 --> 00:01:21,298
Why? I'd searched a simplified map that mostly knows genre and style.

19
00:01:21,498 --> 00:01:24,612
On the full map, empty spots only tied with just asking.

20
00:01:25,032 --> 00:01:26,963
What won? Using the map as a compass.

21
00:01:27,163 --> 00:01:32,035
The A.I. writes an idea, the map says which games it's too close to, and it moves away.

22
00:01:32,235 --> 00:01:34,838
Now only about eighty Steam games were more unusual.

23
00:01:35,038 --> 00:01:36,959
And a second map it never saw agreed.

24
00:01:37,379 --> 00:01:38,349
Here's what came out.

25
00:01:38,549 --> 00:01:41,225
Captain a village's church bell ringers in eighteen twenty.

26
00:01:41,425 --> 00:01:44,746
Be the hidden prompter, whispering lines to actors who forgot them.

27
00:01:44,946 --> 00:01:47,319
Perform a silent film's sound effects live.

28
00:01:47,519 --> 00:01:50,871
Write the first great English dictionary before the money runs out.

29
00:01:51,291 --> 00:01:56,665
But unusual isn't never made, so another A.I. searched the web for all sixteen ideas.

30
00:01:56,865 --> 00:01:59,533
Ten turned up nothing similar, though most checks were quick.

31
00:01:59,733 --> 00:02:01,005
Five had something close.

32
00:02:01,205 --> 00:02:05,367
And one already existed: a Viking rowing rhythm game called Ragnarock.

33
00:02:05,787 --> 00:02:07,919
So, can A.I. come up with a new idea?

34
00:02:08,119 --> 00:02:14,151
With the map as its compass, its typical idea was more unusual than ninety-nine point nine percent of Steam.

35
00:02:14,351 --> 00:02:17,609
The map didn't invent them. It just kept saying: that's been done.
